\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Finite and measurable probability laws}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{probability}

\p{We formulate the first language-model objects on finite sets because a
finite vocabulary and a bounded token sequence admit ordinary sums. This
keeps normalization and conditioning explicit. The finite-sequence interface
matches \citet{sec. 3, Sequence modelling}{phuong2022formal}.}

\p{States, observations, and tool outputs need not be finite. In that setting
#{\Delta(X)} is replaced by probability measures on a declared measurable
space and conditional laws are probability kernels. No later argument may
silently pass between the finite and measurable settings.}
